WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.160
THE STATE OF HTC:
Once upon a time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:07.720
HTC was one of the biggest smartphone&nbsp;
producers in the world. In fact, in 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:13.600
they were the 3rd largest phone manufacturer in&nbsp;
the entire world only beaten by Apple and Samsung&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:19.440
themselves. Within the US, HTC was actually even&nbsp;
ahead of Samsung as the go to Android phone maker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:27.600
with a market share of 24%. But ever since then,&nbsp;
it’s only been straight down for HTC. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:34.920
you now need 2 leading zeros to describe their&nbsp;
global market share of just 0.09%. Even the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:41.520
revived Nokia brand has 5 times the market share.&nbsp;
Within just 4 years, HTC’s revenue dropped from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:47.440
$6 billion to less than $2 billion. And their&nbsp;
net income? Well, they went from barely being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:53.240
profitable to heavily being in the red losing&nbsp;
hundreds of millions every single year. HTC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:58.880
would eventually end up selling a large portion of&nbsp;
themselves to Google out of desperation, but this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:04.240
too hasn’t led anywhere. Things are so bad that&nbsp;
rumors about HTC shutting down their smartphone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:09.400
business altogether have flying around for years&nbsp;
now. It’s easy to write off this massive decline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:16.000
as just a result of Apple and Samsung dominating&nbsp;
the market and becoming duopoly, but there’s a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:23.080
more to story. You see, much of HTC’s decline can&nbsp;
be explained by HTC’s own decisions but not in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:28.240
way that you might think. With a company like&nbsp;
Yahoo or Xerox, for example, their leadership&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:33.760
simply made the wrong choice altogether. With HTC&nbsp;
though, their leadership often actually made the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:39.960
right choices but the competition simply made even&nbsp;
better choices. In fact, during the early part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:45.600
their decline, HTC was actually putting up put&nbsp;
record numbers year after year. It’s just that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:51.960
the competition was putting even more impressive&nbsp;
record numbers. So, it’s not necessarily that HTC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:57.760
went down but rather that they simply couldn’t&nbsp;
keep up. The smartphone industry continued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:03.760
to explode without them, and over time, this&nbsp;
destroyed them from the outside in. So join me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:15.782
as we take a look back at the monstrous rise and&nbsp;
fall of one of the OG smartphone producers: HTC.

00:02:15.782 --> 00:02:17.400
FLAWED INNOVATION:
Taking a look back, the story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:24.800
HTC can be traced all the way back to May 15, 1997&nbsp;
to Tawian, a full decade ahead of the iPhone. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:30.720
name HTC Corporation actually stands for High&nbsp;
Tech Computer Corporation and HTC definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:35.120
tried to live up to this name. In fact, most of&nbsp;
their efforts over the years went into trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:40.720
to innovate and create the next big thing. For&nbsp;
example, in 1998, they would begin designing one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:46.120
of the world’s first touch screen smartphones. And&nbsp;
they weren’t just trying to make a smarter mobile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:51.400
phone either. Their real goal was to make a full&nbsp;
fledged handheld computer that ran windows, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:58.040
they would succeed too. In 2002, they would launch&nbsp;
the world’s first Windows powered smartphone. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:03.360
not only did they launch the world’s first Windows&nbsp;
smartphone, they also launched the world’s first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:09.880
Android smartphone: the T-Mobile G1 also known&nbsp;
as the HTC Dream. HTC wasn’t just innovating with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:15.560
smartphones either. They were very much pushing&nbsp;
forward the entire mobile industry. In 2005,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:20.320
they helped popularize 3G and just a few years&nbsp;
later, they would help popularize 4G with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:26.800
launch of America’s first 4G phone: the HTC Evo&nbsp;
4G. Hearing all this, I don’t think you’d be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:32.800
surprised to hear that HTC was named as one of the&nbsp;
most innovative companies in the world in 2010. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:37.840
a modern tech company in a rapidly evolving and&nbsp;
highly competitive industry such as smartphones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:43.080
you would think that this is precisely the mindset&nbsp;
that you would need. The mindset to constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:49.840
innovate and push forward. After all, isn’t&nbsp;
that what Apple did? Well, not exactly. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:54.920
Apple’s real innovations were not in technology&nbsp;
itself. With most smartphone technologies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:59.680
Android usually does it first and it’s not till&nbsp;
years later that Apple does the same thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.960
but it’s completely different when Apple does&nbsp;
it because Apple’s real innovation is often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:10.400
not in the technology itself but actually in&nbsp;
how the technology is delivered. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:14.360
how Apple made Face ID so reliable that&nbsp;
you could seamlessly use it dozens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:19.120
times everyday and not even notice. How they&nbsp;
made bite size bluetooth headphones that not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.840
only provided a strong listening experience but&nbsp;
also practical battery life. How they make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:29.200
a breeze to take professional level pictures&nbsp;
and videos. Apple’s real innovative power is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:34.560
not in creating new technologies but rather&nbsp;
taking existing technologies and making them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:41.320
effortless. HTC on the other hand was truly just&nbsp;
working on creating new technologies. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:46.920
the HTC Evo 3D was the first phone to leverage&nbsp;
dual rear cameras, something that was copied by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:56.200
Apple. The HTC Thunderbolt was the first LTE&nbsp;
enabled phone, years before Apple. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.200 --> 00:05:03.080
HTC One M7 was the first aluminum unibody phone,&nbsp;
something that’s oddly familiar to the iPhone 6.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:08.200
But while these innovations may have spoken to&nbsp;
tech enthusiasts, the same cannot be said about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:13.320
the general public. When it came to the general&nbsp;
public, Apple and Samsung’s approach of packaging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:18.480
technologies and innovating experiences was&nbsp;
simply far more effective. But as we know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.280
Apple themselves weren’t able to keep&nbsp;
up with this forever. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:27.400
they started dropping the ball. The iPhone 15&nbsp;
for example was likely their most uninspiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:33.640
launch of all time but none of this is really&nbsp;
hurting Apple because they diversified. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:39.520
not that HTC didn’t try diversifying themselves&nbsp;
but let’s just say, it didn’t go nearly as well.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:48.520
FLAWED DIVERSIFICATION:
iPhone sales have been declining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:54.240
for years now. In fact, Apple doesn’t even want&nbsp;
to reveal how many units they’re moving nowadays.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:59.200
But, this hasn’t hurt their bottomline at all&nbsp;
because they’ve been able to diversify. Instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:04.160
of monetizing the sale of iPhones, Apple has moved&nbsp;
towards monetizing other parts of the smartphone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:09.800
business like the use of the iPhone. Similarly,&nbsp;
Samsung has seen a lot of success as a background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:15.440
player within the smartphone industry. Most people&nbsp;
know Samsung for their Galaxy and Note phones but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:20.400
Samsung is actually one of the biggest background&nbsp;
players within the smartphone industry dominating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:26.520
the display and flash memory markets. In fact,&nbsp;
iPhone screens themselves are made by Samsung. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:32.600
clearly, there’s a lot of money to be made through&nbsp;
strategic diversificaiton and HTC knew. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:35.520
they’ve been trying to make similar&nbsp;
diversification efforts for over a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:41.360
decade now but it hasn’t quite played&nbsp;
out the same way. For example, in 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:45.600
they decided to increase their control over the&nbsp;
hardware space by purchasing S3 Graphics for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:52.000
$300 million. S3 Graphics had made strides within&nbsp;
the 3D industry and held 100s of critical patents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.600
that had been licensed out to Sony, Nintendo,&nbsp;
and Microsoft for their gaming consoles. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:01.000
HTC figured that they could takeover S3&nbsp;
graphics and become a licensing giant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:05.200
within the smartphone graphics industry. Google&nbsp;
was making a similar play around the same time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:10.760
with Motorola. Google would spend $12.5 billion&nbsp;
to acquire Motorola and more specifically their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:16.640
14,600 patents. And they would use these patents&nbsp;
to successfully defend themselves against Apple’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:22.560
lawsuits against Android making the acquisitions&nbsp;
a massive success. HTC was likely hoping that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:27.680
could pull off a similar victory against Apple&nbsp;
but unfortunately for them, the court would end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:33.840
up siding with Apple. And S3 would just end up&nbsp;
fading into oblivion after that. Fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:39.760
HTC was working on other diversification efforts&nbsp;
as well like Beats by Dre. Years before Apple got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:45.920
involved, HTC invested $300 million into Beats in&nbsp;
2011 and acquired a majority stake in the company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:51.240
But as HTC’s fundamentals started declining, they&nbsp;
decided to cash in on this investment prematurely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:56.680
selling for a mere $56 million profit. Just&nbsp;
1 year after this, Apple would end up buying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.680 --> 00:08:04.640
Beats for $3 billion. Talk about bad timing from&nbsp;
HTC. Obviously, HTC didn’t have much luck with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:08.560
the music industry but one industry where&nbsp;
they were not only early but stuck around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:13.880
till the end was streaming. HTC actually made&nbsp;
two separate streaming related investments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:18.880
the first of which was a game streaming company&nbsp;
called OnLive. OnLive was basically an early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:24.080
version of Google Stadia and it already had big&nbsp;
players backing them including BT, Warner Bros,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:29.320
and AT&amp;T. Like Google Stadia though, OnLive&nbsp;
didn’t end up working out and they would end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:34.440
up shutting down in early 2015. Fortunately,&nbsp;
HTC’s other streaming related acquisition was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:39.000
going much better: Saffron Digital. Saffron&nbsp;
Digital specialized in creating and hosting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:46.040
the infrastructure used by the NBA, NFL, NHL,&nbsp;
Paramount, NBC, and Sony. But after getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:51.400
stung by OnLive, HTC would decide to derisk&nbsp;
when it came to streaming, and Saffron would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:56.240
end up getting acquired by NeuLion. Honestly, it&nbsp;
was probably a good idea to focus back on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:08.421
core product and start turning things around,&nbsp;
but unfortunately, this turnaround never came.

00:09:08.421 --> 00:09:09.600
FLAWED POSITIONING:
More than innovating the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:14.880
wrong things and failed diversification efforts,&nbsp;
likely the number one factor that dragged down HTC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:20.600
was lackluster marketing. And again, just like&nbsp;
their innovation and diversification efforts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:26.520
it’s not that HTC was against marketing. In fact,&nbsp;
they were all for marketing, budgeting as much as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:32.200
$1 billion for individual campaigns. They locked&nbsp;
in A list celebrities like Robert Downey Jr,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:38.240
paying him $12 million for just a 2 minute&nbsp;
commercial. But while HTC onboard with all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:44.480
of this, I think they made one fatal flaw which&nbsp;
was considering brand positioning more carefully.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:50.080
It seems that HTC approached the whole branding&nbsp;
side of things a bit too logically. They basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:55.240
thought, ok we have a solid set of products and&nbsp;
reasonable prices. If we simply put this infront&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.240 --> 00:10:00.280
of people with huge marketing spend, we should&nbsp;
be selling out in no time. But the reality is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:05.720
that marketing isn’t so logical especially in&nbsp;
the modern age. More and more people tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:11.520
prioritize how a brand makes them feel as opposed&nbsp;
to what a brand is offering them on paper. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:16.520
precisely why Apple’s marketing is so effective.&nbsp;
They’re not selling the iPhone spec sheet or its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:21.600
value proposition. They’re selling feelings. The&nbsp;
feeling of premiumness with their material choices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:26.600
and sleek designs. The feeling of exclusivity&nbsp;
with their high prices and strategic market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:32.520
positioning. And most importantly, the feeling&nbsp;
of inclusion with iMessage and Facetime and now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:38.960
even phone calls. These feelings are what sell&nbsp;
iPhones and Galaxies, not the spec sheet. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:43.880
this is a massive departure from how things used&nbsp;
to be before 2000 as people usually just made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:48.320
purchase decisions based on who could give them&nbsp;
the best bang for the buck. That’s why companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:54.280
like Sony and Panasonic were so popular. They made&nbsp;
strong reliable products for a reasonable price,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:59.600
and that’s why people bought their products, as&nbsp;
simple as that. But trying to apply this legacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:05.080
strategy to the smartphone industry simply didn’t&nbsp;
work and I think this is what really screwed over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:10.560
HTC on all fronts. In terms of innovation, HTC&nbsp;
was looking to buff up the spec sheet and pack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:15.600
in the best technology possible when the real&nbsp;
solution was to create the best experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:20.760
possible. In terms of diversification, they&nbsp;
stuck to fundamentals. S3 Graphics for example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:25.960
seemed like a safe patent play, and they sold off&nbsp;
Beats because they got a reasonable good offer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:31.080
There was no way to expect that Apple would&nbsp;
pay $3 billion for Beats because it wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:36.880
fundamentally worth that much. But Apple was ok&nbsp;
with paying that for non fundamental reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:41.360
And in terms of marketing, HTC was very much&nbsp;
willing to put their money where their mouth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:47.280
was but their objective approach simply didn’t&nbsp;
resonate with the general public. The reality was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:53.320
that HTC always trying to make the objectively&nbsp;
right choice, but the truth is that it’s often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:58.960
the intangibles that make a company truly&nbsp;
successful. And without those intangibles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:05.120
HTC simply couldn’t stand the test of time. With&nbsp;
historically high interest rates, the appeal bills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:10.720
and bonds is the higher than ever, yet traditional&nbsp;
brokerages haven’t really adapted with the times.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:15.840
They still expect people to use clunky bond&nbsp;
tables, scroll through long overwhleming lists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:22.480
and even use virtual keyboards till recently.&nbsp;
And that’s why we created our own modern bond&nbsp;&nbsp;

